45417] [17592186549935 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549935 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549935 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549935 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n}{2}x)\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of the $4\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5, & x\\in [0,2\\pi ) \\\\ -2, & x\\in [-2\\pi ,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{3}.$\n\n${b}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549935 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14280c74-af6e-5d18-9712-69501b60ddc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549935 #edb/ref 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2\\pi .$ Also, $f(x)=5$ for $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $f(x)=-2$ for $x\\in [-2\\pi ,0).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{-2\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{-2\\pi }^{0}(-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{2\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3}){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x)]}_{-2\\pi }^{0}+\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3}){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3\\pi ))-\\frac{5}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }(1-(-1))-\\frac{5}{3\\pi }((-1)-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }+\\frac{10}{3\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549959 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549959 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341861" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549959 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549959 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-4x$ for $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{2}.$\n\n${b}_{2}=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46598e7e-81d3-578b-80cc-94d59f9707e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549959 #edb/ref 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=\\pi $ and $f(x)=-4x$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{\\pi }x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-4x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{2}{[(-4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(-4)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{2}((-4\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-(4\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi ))-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{2}((-4\\pi )\\cdot 1-(4\\pi )\\cdot 1)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(4\\pi -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (4\\pi -0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549978 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549978 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341863" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549978 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549978 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=x+1$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{2}.$\n\n${a}_{2}=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549978 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c3f231c-8d0c-511e-b4f9-0d81c5e6659f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549978 #edb/ref 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=x+1$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(uv\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }{[(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)]}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )]-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\pi }){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)]}_{-2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549980 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341780" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549980 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549980 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in [-L,L)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({{field-1}})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({{field-2}}).\n$$\n\nThe $7$th sine coefficient of the series is\n\n$$\n{b}_{7}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $F(x)=x$ and $L=2\\pi ,$ the $7$th sine coefficient becomes\n\n$$\n{b}_{7}={{field-4}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-5}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549980 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "903d90ff-4e96-59f7-912a-24572f1e9b78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549980 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7$th sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{7} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=2\\pi $ and $f(x)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{7} & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{2\\pi }x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7}{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549993 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-3x+3$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc08f5e3-6f0e-55df-87af-7552a06bb9df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549993 #edb/ref 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=-3x+3$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(-3x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+3x]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}+3\\cdot 2)-(-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{2}+3\\cdot (-2))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-(-12)) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550007 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550007 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341873" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550007 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550007 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=2x+7$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\n${a}_{1}=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5dc675f-f1b9-58e9-a567-64d8cd60aaaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550007 #edb/ref 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and $f(x)=2x+7$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(2x+7)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+7\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}(uv\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{3}{\\pi }{[(2x+7)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{3}{\\pi }[13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )]-\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\cdot 0-\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{\\pi }){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{18}{{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )]) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{\\pi }^{2}}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550020 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550020 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550020 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in [-L,L)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({{field-1}})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({{field-2}}).\n$$\n\nThe $6$th cosine coefficient of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{6}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $F(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ and $L=5,$ the $6$th cosine coefficient becomes\n\n$$\n{a}_{6}={{field-4}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-5}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb364528-cf72-5941-b3b8-fc021ca9ace0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550020 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $6$th cosine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=5$ and $f(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {\\int }_{-5}^{5}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
